WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.760
At the age of 9 he knew what he wanted to
do with his life, and he never wavered from

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.760
his goal.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.020
Now, he’s one of the world’s most famous
scientists.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.469
He’s earned the title of “Most Powerful
Nerd In The Universe” from National Public

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:15.969
Radio and the title of “The World’s Sexiest
Astrophysicist” from People Magazine.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:20.589
He was instrumental in cutting down the number
of planets in our solar system, and in doing

00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:26.529
so upset entire generations who had come to
love Pluto as the quirky, smallest planet.

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:32.520
And who is this icon of science that’s recognizable
by the general populace?

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:38.570
… Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:49.770
Early Life

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.310
Tyson was born in New York City in 1959 and
grew up in the Bronx.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.640
His mother worked for the U.S. Department
of Health, Education, and Welfare, and his

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.870
father worked for the City of New York.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:02.610
Tyson was a star student at the Bronx High
School of Science.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:06.760
Well before beginning at the Bronx High School
of Science, Tyson had decided that science

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.280
was the path he wanted to follow in life.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.030
When he was in elementary school, Tyson’s
class took a field trip to the Hayden Planetarium.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.890
While there, he felt that he was literally
called to a career in science.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.920
"...so strong was that imprint [of the night
sky] that I'm certain that I had no choice

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.270
in the matter, that in fact, the universe
called me."

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.420
Tyson immediately began committing time to
his newfound interest - some might say obsession

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.670
- and his time in high school showed his dedication
to the study of the stars.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.090
While attending the Bronx High School of Science,
Tyson was a wrestler but he devoted much time

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.120
to his studies.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.060
He was the editor of the Physical Science
Journal, and regular classes weren’t enough

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.210
for him...he took classes at the very place
that sparked his interest in astrophysics

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.290
- the Hayden Planetarium.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.940
After school, he spent time observing the
sky, moon, and stars from the roof of his

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.180
New York home.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.840
His dedication to science and academics during
his teenage years grabbed the attention of

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.630
some prominent people.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.060
One, you may have heard of - Carl Sagan.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.879
When it came time for Tyson to apply to college,
Cornell was among the schools he applied to.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:14.349
Sagan was a professor at Cornell, and the
admissions office made sure he was sent a

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:18.969
copy of Tyson’s application after they relieved
the talent and potential that was within this

00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:20.379
aspiring scientist.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:24.629
Sagan was duly impressed by what Tyson had
written in his application, so much so that

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:27.529
he invited Tyson up to spend a day at Cornell.

00:02:27.529 --> 00:02:32.579
Tyson remembers that day in 1975 as one of
the most formative in his life.

00:02:32.579 --> 00:02:38.120
Sagan died in 1996, and Tyson came into possession
of his datebook which includes the notation

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.750
“Neil Tyson” on the square for December
20, 1975.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:46.480
The two science-minded men spent the day together,
touring Cornell’s labs and discussing the

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.049
mysteries of the universe.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:52.340
Sagan gifted Tyson with a copy of the book
“The Cosmic Connection,” inscribing it

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.799
to indicate his prediction and hope for Tyson’s
career - “To A Future Astronomer.”

00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:02.000
Tyson never forgot what he learned from Sagan
that day, and the lessons he took back to

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.749
the Bronx with him were about far more than
the scientific method.

00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:09.299
“I already knew I wanted to become a scientist,
but that afternoon I learned from Carl the

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:11.379
kind of person I wanted to become.

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:13.739
He reached out to me and to countless others.

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:17.590
Inspiring so many of us to study, teach, and
do science.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.999
Science is a cooperative enterprise, spanning
the generations.”

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:25.909
A few months after his visit to Cornell, Tyson
graduated from the Bronx High School of Science.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:30.840
Even though his visit with Sagan had a profound
impact on his life, Tyson opted not to attend

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.840
Cornell.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.439
But as an excellent student, he had no shortage
of prominent schools to choose from.

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:38.370
Though Cornell wasn’t the right fit for
him, Harvard was.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.689
Tyson entered Harvard as an undergrad in the
fall of 1976.

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:45.680
As a student, he paid his tuition partially
through grants and scholarships, but also

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.700
through a work study program.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.879
Part of Tyson’s work requirement was to
clean bathrooms on campus - including those

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:53.089
of other students.

00:03:53.089 --> 00:03:57.120
On a Larry King Live appearance, he described
the experience as “a little weird.”

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.730
But he was willing to do it to pay his way
through school and achieve his dreams.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:05.669
Working and studying physics weren’t enough
to occupy the active and curious Tyson, though.

00:04:05.669 --> 00:04:10.669
He was on the crew team and the wrestling
team while he was at Harvard, and was also

00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:12.330
a jazz, ballet, and Latin dancer.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:16.859
He graduated from Harvard in 1980 with a degree
in physics, and kept his academic career moving

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:21.060
forward with an acceptance to the University
of Texas for a Master’s Program.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.139
He completed his M.A. in astronomy 1983, and
he intended to pursue a doctorate from the

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.139
college.

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:31.710
But he hit a roadblock, and Tyson himself
admits that the roadblock was largely of his

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:32.889
own making.

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:36.639
Looking back, he has admitted that he didn’t
devote as much time to doing lab research

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:40.400
during his graduate years as he should have
and could have, and this prevented him from

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.360
being able to go right from his Master’s
program to earning a doctoral degree.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.310
He even had professors tell him to stop working
in the field of astronomy.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:52.610
At one point, struggling as a student and
broke, Tyson considered a very different job

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.580
than that of professor or museum director.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.419
He thought about becoming an exotic dancer.

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:01.289
He even went so far as to go to a show to
see if he could do it.

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:05.050
When the dancers came out and their skimpy
undergarments were lit up as they gyrated

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:09.639
to a rendition of “Great Balls of Fire,”
Tyson decided that no matter how broke he

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:12.889
was, exotic dancing wasn’t for him.

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:17.020
Thankfully for the scientific community, Tyson’s
break from academics was short-lived.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.960
With his undergraduate achievements and a
start on a graduate degree, he was able to

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.539
land a teaching job at the University of Maryland,
and eventually found his way back onto the

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:27.669
doctoral track in the Ivy Leagues.

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:32.669
In 1991, fifteen years after scrubbing toilets
to pay for his undergraduate degree, Tyson

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:37.139
graduated from Columbia University with a
doctorate in astrophysics.

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:44.919
He then stayed in the Ivy League system, moving
to Princeton for a postdoctoral position.

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:52.520
Scientific Career

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.060
While he was earning his degrees and moving
his way up the academic ladder, Tyson was

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.919
publishing articles and writing a column for
StarDate, a science magazine published by

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:03.909
the University of Texas.

00:06:03.909 --> 00:06:08.990
While he was attending Columbia, he worked
under a professor who was supportive of Tyson’s

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.280
ambitions and intellect, applying for grants
that gave Tyson access to the international

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:14.500
astronomy community.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:19.219
Tyson traveled throughout Europe, attending
meetings and learning from the best and brightest

00:06:19.219 --> 00:06:21.180
in the field around the world.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.900
And Tyson’s own star was starting to rise...
soon after graduating from Columbia he landed

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:33.120
his dream job … a position at the very Hayden
Planetarium where his dreams had started.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.139
He started out as a staff scientist.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:38.439
It was only one year before he was named Acting
Director of the planetarium.

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:44.480
Then, only one more year until he was formally
named the director of the Planetarium.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.870
Early on in his tenure as director, he made
updating and modernizing the facility a keystone

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.449
of his work at Hayden.

00:06:51.449 --> 00:06:56.749
The project was a 200 million dollar undertaking
that gave the public a modernized, inviting

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.509
look at the vastness of the universe.

00:06:59.509 --> 00:07:02.949
And his work extended well beyond the Planetarium.

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:07.789
In 2001, he received a presidential appointment
to work on policies related to the future

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:09.469
of the U.S. space industry.

00:07:09.469 --> 00:07:14.150
After that, he was part of a commission to
work on the future of space exploration.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:18.980
His service on these commissions was so impressive
that NASA gave him the “Distinguished Public

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:19.980
Service Medal.”

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.349
Tyson’s scientific work, as well as decisions
he made about the Hayden Planetarium put him

00:07:24.349 --> 00:07:28.189
into the spotlight for the general public,
well beyond just those in the scientific community.

00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:32.380
He became a household name because of one
decision in particular.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.380
What did he do?

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:37.349
He removed the planet of Pluto from the Planetarium’s
solar system display.

00:07:37.349 --> 00:07:41.830
Schoolchildren, parents, and scientists alike
were not impressed with his decision.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:45.389
And soon became about more than that one Planetarium’s
display.

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:50.310
Pluto’s fate was taken up by an international
organization...the International Astronomical

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:51.310
Union.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:54.919
In August of 2006 the organization made their
agreement with Tyson’s stance on Pluto official

00:07:54.919 --> 00:08:00.819
when they deemed Pluto a dwarf planet, officially
knocking our solar system down to only 8 planets.

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:05.290
So what does Tyson maintain is so wrong with
Pluto?

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.979
For one, it has the potential to grow a tail.

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:08.979
What?

00:08:08.979 --> 00:08:09.979
Yes, a tail.

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:12.020
Here’s the explanation from Tyson himself:

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:16.611
“So Pluto is not only the littlest planet,
all right, that alone shouldn't hurt it, but

00:08:16.611 --> 00:08:18.790
more than half of its volume is ice.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.159
No other planet has that.

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:24.400
So if you moved it to where Earth is right
now, heat from the sun would evaporate the

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.470
ice and it would grow a tail.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.270
That's no kind of behavior for a planet!”

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.540
Add to that, Pluto’s orbit is much more
elongated than that of the other planets,

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:35.990
and it’s so small that other planets have
moons that are larger than Pluto.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:43.690
Though the Pluto decision was perhaps the
most famous - some would say infamous - scientific

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:48.860
assertion of Tyson’s career, he has published
articles, essays, and books that discuss much

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:51.290
more than the demotion of a planet.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:55.480
He wrote for Natural History magazine in the
90s, and his columns were turned into two

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.870
different books, Death By Black Hole and Astrophysics
for People in a Hurry.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:04.050
He chose the pen name of Merlin for himself
when he wrote a column in the Star Date magazine,

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:08.930
and those columns were also published as books.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:16.850
Pop Culture Figure

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:21.190
Tyson hasn’t just used publications to spread
the word about science and the universe.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:25.190
He’s hosted TV shows, regularly appears
as a guest on other shows, and has his own

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:27.350
podcast called “StarTalk.”

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:32.680
He hosted NovaScience Now in the mid-2000s,
raising his profile even further to Americans.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.990
He was so popular as a host, that in 2014
the network brought him back to delve even

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.290
further into science for the American people.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:44.350
The 2014 show gave Tyson the opportunity to
build on the work of a man who had impacted

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.940
him early on in his life...Carl Sagan.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:47.940
The show?

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.030
Cosmos, a new version of the popular one that
Sagan had hosted in 1980, based on his book

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:53.030
of the same name.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:57.130
At the time of its airing in 1980, Cosmos
was the most-watched show in the United States.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.300
If there was anyone who could live up to those
kinds of expectations, it was Tyson.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.570
He didn’t have to go it alone in 2014, though.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:07.930
He had the help of Sagan’s widow, who worked
as a writer for Cosmos, and the two made sure

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:11.720
to include allusions to Sagan and his work
throughout the rebooted series.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:17.190
The series was a success, and over 3 million
Americans put their TV on Fox to catch the

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:22.190
newest science lesson presented by Tyson every
Sunday night for 13 weeks.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:26.800
The Cosmos reboot was undoubtedly successful,
and showed Tyson’s appeal to the general

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:27.800
populace.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:32.650
But his appeal in the media has gone well
beyond direct, serious science.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:36.180
He’s appeared as a guest star on Family
Guy, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, and as a character

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:37.810
in New Ice Age.

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:42.530
The Big Bang Theory, with its nerdy cast,
of course welcomed Tyson on as a guest.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.470
He even made an appearance on Avenged Sevenfold’s
album The Stage.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.970
He didn’t sing, but instead delivered a
monologue during one of the band’s songs.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:54.320
A musical appearance that was rumored to happen,
but never did, was that Tyson would appear

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.390
on an album with Wu Tang Clan member GZA.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:01.020
Though the appearance didn’t happen, it
speaks to Tyson’s breadth of talents and

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:05.490
popularity that many people thought it was
a very probable thing for Tyson to do.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:09.350
While the album didn’t happen, Tyson and
GZA did appear together when GZA rapped on

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:10.350
StarTalk.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.910
Even the Daily Show has regularly featured
Tyson to comment on science matters, and as

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:18.900
he did with Pluto, Tyson made no bones about
sharing his thoughts on what he thought needed

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:19.900
correction.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.260
Live on-air, he informed Jon Stewart:

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.640
"The beginning of your program?

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.510
The Earth is spinning in the wrong direction.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:25.510
I'm sorry.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.390
I had to get that off my chest."

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:31.870
The Earth on the credits was spinning clockwise,
when Earth really spins counter-clockwise.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:35.640
Though Tyson pointed it out to Stewart and
the entire viewing audience, it wasn’t until

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:40.690
Trevor Noah took over as host in 2015 that
the show made a permanent correction to the

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.480
Earth’s spinning direction.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.460
Tyson was excited about the change, and tweeted
out his thanks to the Daily Show.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:49.600
The Daily Show’s globe isn’t the only
correction that Tyson’s scientific knowledge

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.690
has led to in the realm of pop culture.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:56.260
Remember the 1997 blockbuster movie Titanic?

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:58.820
Tyson saw it - and he wasn’t impressed.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.160
Near the end, after the ship has sunk, Rose
is lying on a board in the freezing Atlantic

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:03.160
Ocean.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.130
She’s gazing up at the night sky, and the
audience can see what she see’s - the stars

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:08.670
in the sky over the ocean.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:12.610
When Tyson saw the scene, he noticed something
the rest of us didn’t.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.630
The stars weren’t the right ones and weren’t
the right arrangement for the year 1912, at

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.920
the latitude and longitude where the Titanic
sunk.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:22.290
So, as with the Daily Show, Tyson let director
David Cameron know.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:26.340
According to Cameron, Tyson sent him a “snarky
email” on the subject.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:30.620
Fifteen years after the film was first released,
Cameron took Tyson’s suggestion and adjusted

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.380
the night sky in that scene to be accurate.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.320
Titanic and the Daily Show weren’t enough
for Tyson.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.770
He’s even had a hand in keeping Superman
scientifically accurate.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:43.250
DC Comics wanted to feature the world’s
most famous astrophysicist in a comic, but

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.760
Tyson wouldn’t do it unless he could be
sure of the accuracy.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.620
With the distance Krypton is supposed to be
from the Earth, Tyson he would never be able

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.680
to help Superman spot it, so what did he do?

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.950
He did his research found an actual red star
to serve as Krypton, and crafted a story whereby

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:03.150
Superman would combine all the telescopes
on Earth to be able to see Krypton.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:06.300
No media format has been untouched by Tyson.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:10.670
The man is everywhere, spreading the word
of science, and the people of the world are

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:11.670
listening.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.200
Creators and producers take his advice, and
seek him out for appearances.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:21.450
Among scientists, he is unique in this kind
of stardom.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:28.540
Personal Life

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:32.510
A serious scientist and a pop culture figure
would seem to make any person interesting

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:33.510
enough.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:37.190
But Tyson has hobbies and interests that go
well beyond the mysteries of space and the

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:38.600
sharing of science.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.070
He and his wife, Alice - who he met in a physics
class - are both foodies.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:45.780
Tyson has said they probably spend too much
money on food, but living in Manhattan they

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:50.310
have so many restaurants to explore and so
many food options to try out they take every

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.710
opportunity they can to enjoy new foods.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.700
Tyson is a also wine connoisseur.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.620
His love of science plays a big role in his
enjoyment of wine, helping him to understand

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.670
why different wines taste different and how
they can be best served.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:07.300
Like much else, Tyson explains these concepts
simply so that non-scientists can understand

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:08.300
them, too.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.280
“The lower-than-32-degrees freezing point
of liquids that contain alcohol, like wine,

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:16.260
allows you to chill a bottle of white wine
to sub-freezing temperatures without it freezing,

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:17.750
before you bring it a dinner party.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:21.890
The wine warms up during the travel time to
just the right temperature in time for dinner.”

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:27.920
And a rather well-known scientist is the source
of Tyson’s favorite quote about food and

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:28.920
wine...Galileo.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:32.850
"The sun, with all those planets revolving
around it and dependent on it, can still ripen

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.560
a bunch of grapes as if it had nothing else
in the universe to do."

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.980
Food, wine, physics … what’s left for
him to care about and learn about?

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.230
Calligraphy, for one.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:46.500
Tyson can create beautiful lettered art with
a feather pen, but is especially passionate

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:47.840
about fountain pens.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:52.220
He loves them so much that his wife spent
6,000 dollars to buy him a Japanese-made pen

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.690
as a gift when he finalized the contract for
his autobiography.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:00.850
He even gave an entire interview to the New
York Times on the pen and his love of calligraphy.

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:05.160
In the interview, he described his own mathematical
theory behind how you can tell if a pen is

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.570
perfect for calligraphy.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.911
Having to dip the pen to refresh the ink after
6 to 8 words mean you’re writing with a

00:15:09.911 --> 00:15:17.200
beautiful rhythm, he says.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.460
Conclusion

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:23.510
He’s passionate about life and the finer
things in it.

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.730
He’s passionate about science, and the world
knows it.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:29.570
With all that he’s accomplished in his life
and the celebrity status he’s achieved,

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:32.500
it would be easy to excuse Tyson for having
a large ego.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:33.500
But he doesn’t.

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:36.840
In fact, he takes special pains to point out
the danger of ego:

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:41.470
“If your ego starts out, 'I am important,
I am big, I am special,' you're in for some

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:45.440
disappointments when you look around at what
we've discovered about the universe.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:46.770
No, you're not big.

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:47.770
No, you're not.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:49.600
You're small in time and in space.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:54.230
And you have this frail vessel called the
human body that's limited on Earth.”

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:58.120
Perhaps this attitude is a large part of his
success and why he is so beloved.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.730
For all his intelligence and his contributions
to the study of space, Neil DeGrasse Tyson

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:33.010
remains firmly planted with two feet on Earth.

